.TH CHTTPSVR_RESP_WRITE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_resp_write \- append bytes to the response body
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_resp_write(chttpsvr_resp *resp,
                                   const void *data, size_t len);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Appends len bytes of data to the internal buffer of the response body.
The buffer grows automatically by overflow-safe doubling: each
reallocation at least doubles the capacity, and a guard keeps the
computation from wrapping a size_t around.  The function returns
ccol_not_enough_memory when len plus the current body length would
overflow a size_t, or when the underlying allocator returns NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args when resp is NULL, or when data is NULL and len is
                  above 0.
ccol_not_enough_memory when the body length would overflow a size_t,
                       or when the allocator cannot serve the request.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
For a request whose method is CHTTP_HEAD, whenever the handler wrote a
body, the server reports the real collected body length in the
Content-Length response header, which is the value a GET would
report, as RFC 7231 4.3.2 requires.  A HEAD handler that writes no
body reports only the Content-Length it set with
chttpsvr_resp_set_header(3), and none otherwise.  The server never
writes the body bytes themselves to the wire for such a request, so
a handler that CHTTP_HEAD can reach, either explicitly or through a
CHTTP_ANY registration, needs no special case for the method before
it calls this function.

The server drops the body in the same way, whatever the request
method, when the final status code of the response is a 1xx
(informational), a 204 or a 304, because RFC 9110 6.4.1, 15.2.1 and
15.4.5 forbid a body on all three; the bytes you write with this
function for those codes are silently held back from the wire as
well.  None of the three carries an automatic Content-Length header:
a 304 carries the Content-Length that the handler set with
chttpsvr_resp_set_header(3), and none otherwise.  See
chttpsvr_resp_set_status(3).
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_resp_write_str (3),
.BR chttpsvr_resp_printf (3),
